{"seq":7,"id":"01M6K5BZWW2G52QJM6PV2BWFRW","cdr":"CDR-0002","attempt":"CDR-0002-A1","type":"agent.result","actor":{"kind":"agent","id":"implementer","adapter":"claude"},"at":"2026-11-04T11:33:36.284Z","payload":{"role":"implementer","adapter":"claude","result_sha256":"0339611681754905483e69fc69daeef344c2a79f16325ba46074936806780fc1","result":{"summary":"I changed parse.ParseAmount in parse/parse.go so it rejects invalid amounts instead of returning a wrong value with a nil error. I did not run the tests (no shell tool in this role); the reasoning below is from reading the code against parse/parse_cdr0002_test.go and parse/parse_test.go. After trimming spaces and an optional leading '-', the amount must be digits, optionally followed by '.' and one or two digits. Anything else returns an error: empty input, letters, trailing junk like \"12.3x\", a lone \"-\", \"12.\" or \"1.234\". The strconv.ParseInt error is no longer ignored, and amounts too large to hold in cents now return an out-of-range error. Valid values are unchanged: \"12.34\" gives 1234, \"-5\" gives -500, \"0.5\" gives 50 and \"-1.50\" gives -150. Neither signature changed. ParseLine already returned ParseAmount's error, so it now fails on bad amounts without any edit. A small helper, isDigits, was added.","assumptions":["Spaces around the amount are still trimmed as before, so ParseLine(\"acct , 3.25\") in the existing test still parses.","Trailing dots (\"12.\"), leading dots (\".5\"), a '+' sign and a lone \"-\" are invalid because they do not match [-]digits[.digits].","\"-0\" is accepted and gives 0."],"risks":["Callers that relied on the old lenient parsing (\".5\", \"12.\" or garbage turning into 0) now get errors.","Error messages are new text and not sentinel errors, so callers can't match on a specific error with errors.Is."],"disputes":[]}},"cost":{"tokens_in":8,"tokens_out":2004,"seconds":21.696320916,"usd":0.12653899999999998},"signer":"ads-team:marcello.barile@gmail.com","prev_hash":"4d0bb3